WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
A busy SIEM can fire thousands of alerts a day.

2
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:09.200
If you treated each one as a brand-new mystery, the SOC would grind to a halt by lunchtime.

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.020
The fix isn't working faster. It's working with a repeatable framework.

4
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.570
The word triage comes from emergency medicine.

5
00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:24.700
When many patients arrive at once, a nurse makes a fast sort: who needs help now, who can wait, who's stable.

6
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:35.450
Nobody expects that first sort to be a full diagnosis. SOC triage is the same — a fast verdict with incomplete information, not a complete investigation.

7
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:42.260
First, classify accurately — using a consistent vocabulary instead of a vague gut feeling.

8
00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:49.560
Second, resist fatigue — recognize how repetition wears down your judgment, and build habits that fight it.

9
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:57.190
Third, prioritize by risk, not just by alert type — the same alert can mean very different things depending on context.

10
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.330
That vocabulary has three verdicts.

11
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:06.310
A true positive is a real attack. A false positive matched the rule but isn't malicious.

12
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:14.970
And a benign true positive really happened and really matched — but it was authorized activity. Naming the verdict precisely is half the job.

13
00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:19.190
This is why the framework comes before the technical lessons.

14
00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:27.130
A beginner staring at an alert's dozens of fields has no way to know which ones matter — until they know the decision they're trying to reach.
